   "l": "About to set out to take all-under-heaven and act upon it —\nI see that he does not get it, and that is all.\nAll-under-heaven: a spirit vessel.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Whoever would take all under heaven in hand and work upon it — I see that he will not succeed. All under heaven is a vessel of spirit.",
   "n": "不得已 is three characters that carry two quite different senses, and the reader meets both in two chapters. Here they are 不得 'does not get' closed by the particle 已 'and that is all': the man will not get what he is reaching for. In chapter 30 the same three characters are the set idiom 不得已, 'there was no help for it'. Nothing in the writing distinguishes them — only what the line is doing. Note also that 天下神器 has no verb: Classical Chinese joins a topic to its comment with nothing in between, and the English 'is' is ours. Wang Bi takes the two characters apart before putting them back together: 神, he says, is what has no shape and no fixed quarter; 器 is what is put together and made — and it is precisely a shapeless thing that is nonetheless assembled, he concludes, that gets called a 神器. So each character keeps its own weight in the phrase.",
   "p": true
  },
