   "l": "It cannot be acted upon.\nOne who acts on it ruins it,\none who grasps it loses it.",
   "v": true,
   "n": "可 turns the verb after it passive — 可為 is not 'can act' but 'can be acted upon'. You met this in the first line of the book (道可道). Wang Bi's comment states the doctrine plainly: the ten thousand things have 自然, being so of itself, for their nature, and so they may be gone along with but may not be worked upon, may be let through but may not be gripped. The couplet that follows is the book's favourite shape: two clauses of identical build, differing in two characters — the verb of the first half and the verb of the second."
  },
