   "l": "Therefore of creatures some go ahead, some follow;\nsome breathe warm, some blow cold;\nsome are strong, some are frail;\nsome are broken, some are wrecked.\nAnd so the sage puts away the too-much,\nputs away the too-lavish,\nputs away the too-grand.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Therefore among creatures some lead and some follow, some breathe warm and some blow cold, some are strong and some are frail, some are broken down and some are torn down. And so the sage puts away excess, puts away extravagance, puts away grandeur.",
   "n": "The catalogue of 或 ... 或 says only that creatures come in opposite conditions, which is why they cannot be taken in hand and made uniform. All four pairs end their half-lines on characters that rhymed together in Old Chinese — 隨, 吹, 羸, 隳 — and in Mandarin you can still hear the first pair, suí and chuī; the other two, léi and huī, have drifted apart. The three closing objects are adjectives used as nouns: not 'the excessive thing' but 'the too-much' itself. Text to hold lightly: for 或挫或隳 some editions read 或載或隳, 'some are carried up, some are pulled down', which makes a neater opposition; we keep what our edition prints."
